        <h1
          class="text-5xl font-semibold text-center font-display text-blue-950 sm:text-6xl text-balance"
        >
          The five pillars of the OnTrack Education Framework
        </h1>

        <div class="mt-10 space-y-6 text-left text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
          <p>The OnTrack Education Framework is an Evidence-Centred framework for iterative mastery learning






<ol class="mt-3 space-y-3 list-disc list-outside pl-6">
              <li>
                
                 Mastery: guaranteed standards, either up to standard or not; cover all ULOs for P, no marks
                
              </li>

              <li>
                Active tasks: assessment as learning, learning by doing
                 
              </li>
                 
              <li>
                Trusted evidence: process, discussion, and observation
                
              </li>
              <li>
                Reflexivity: freedom to fail, build feedback literacy, iterative feedback
                
              </li>
<li>
                
                Holistic perspective: capture student's growth, incorporate personas, treat students as whole people
                
              </li>
        
            </ol>

</p>

<h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-left font-display text-blue-750 sm:text-3xl text-balance">1. Achieving Mastery and guaranteeing standards</h2>
<p>
Remove the complexity from your unit design by removing marks. Instead of accumulating marks, students progress by demonstrating competency in undertaking a series of mastery based tasks.


<strong>Why?</strong>
Each task has a specific goal/focus and the only way to pass it is to demonstrate you’ve achieved that. Everything is a yes or no decision so there is no distraction from the purpose of the assessment. There’s no gaming of marks, no shortcuts the students can take, and no need to argue with a student over a given mark.
</p>
<p>
Designing a series of tasks that each focus on a different skill, knowledge or attitude enables you to explicitly guarantee that passing students have met the standards you have set for them on every metric. It also allows you to be very clear about the difference between grade levels and to design explicitly for both passing level students and higher grade students.

<strong>Why?</strong>
Because passing by marks accumulation does not guarantee that students have satisfactorily acquired all the necessary skills.  You wouldn’t give a student a degree if they got a HD in one unit and a fail in another. Why would you pass a student who didn’t demonstrate all the required skills and knowledge within a unit?
In passing a student you are warranting that they are competent to move forward in their education. </p>
